    "chapter_id": "6160241f1a832a8dea12fbf0",
    "chapter_name": "Magnetic Effects of Current",
    "year": "2022",
    "shift": "shift_2"
  },
  "question": {
    "text": "A long solenoid carrying a current produces a magnetic field <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math> along its axis. If the current is doubled and the number of turns per <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>cm</mi></math> is halved, the new value of magnetic field will be equal to"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mi>B</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": true
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "D"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>Magnetic field due to long solenoid along its axis is given as,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>&#956;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>n</mi><mi>I</mi></math>,&nbsp;where&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi></math>&nbsp;is&nbsp;the&nbsp;number of turns in the solenoid per unit length,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>I</mi></math>&nbsp;is current in the solenoid and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#956;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;is a constant called the permeability of free space.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p>According to the question,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>and</mtext><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mfrac><mi>B</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mfenced><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mfrac><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mfenced></mfrac><mo>&#215;</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#8658;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi></math>; means, the&nbsp;magnetic field remains the same.</p></blockquote>"
  }
}